; examples-native harness. `doc.sjon` is a SYMLINK to
; `examples/native.sjon` — the shipped native example is the document
; the gate compiles, so the example cannot rot the way an untested one
; does (pacer 12 D1, `corpus/examples-starter/`; plan 09's corpus list
; ends with "`examples-*` (every shipped example is a symlinked corpus
; case)").
;
; Three verbs, one per promise the example's own header makes. All three
; run against a document whose load already carries one error:
; `impulse-04.it` is NOT shipped beside the example, on purpose, so
; `anchor_unavailable` is in the echo and the two lanes bound to that
; anchor resolve to nothing. That is the documented condition a browser
; host meets too (plan 10: anchors by reference are unresolved in wasm,
; "so a browser host inlines first") and the reason `tempura inline`
; exists. The `tempura` CLI, which has a filesystem resolver, refuses the
; whole build on it and exits 1; the runner has no resolver, so it goes
; on and shows what a build over an unresolved anchor actually produces.
;
; `(export-score)` is the promise that matters most, and it does not need
; the anchor at all: the score is COMPOSE-CLEAN — one `(target …)` per
; target the document drives — every lane kind reaches pacer's
; vocabulary, and the two rounding counts in the header stay at zero.
; `npm/tempura/test/landing.test.js` loads the score this same file
; exports through the real `pacer.wasm`; this golden is the text.
;
; `(build :kind mid)` is REFUSED, and the refusal is the pinned fact:
; the document is arranged around an `it` anchor, and v1 builds a
; document into its own anchor's kind or a native document into either
; (plan 08). A cross-kind build is `build_kind_mismatch`, not a
; transcode.
;
; `(build :kind it)` writes a FRESH module — the anchor carries no inline
; dump, so the anchor's own content is not in the file, which the build
; says in as many words. Rung 1 holds (the bytes re-read and re-write
; identically). Rung 3 does NOT, and this is the only case in the corpus
; where it does not, so here is exactly why: `Doc.eql` compares a
; pass-through pacer form by its SOURCE TEXT, and this document has one —
; `(control :id glow-amt …)`, written on one line. The essence carries
; the form, and restoring it re-prints it through SJON's printer, which
; breaks a seven-keyword form across seven lines. Nothing the document
; MEANS changed; its layout did. Essence-exactness over documents without
; a pass-through form is pinned green by `render-it`, `render-smf` and
; the six `residue-*` cases.
(export-score)
(build :kind mid)
(build :kind it)
